# Metal–Organic Framework Materials for the Separation and Purification of Light Hydrocarbons

## abstract
The separation and purification of light hydrocarbons (LHs) mixtures is one of the most significantly important but energy demanding processes in the petrochemical industry. As an alternative technology to energy intensive traditional separation methods, such as distillation, absorption, extraction, etc., adsorptive separation using selective solid adsorbents could potentially not only lower energy cost but also offer higher efficiency. The need to develop solid materials for the efficiently selective adsorption of LHs molecules, under mild conditions, is therefore of paramount importance and urgency. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), emerging as a relatively new class of porous organic-inorganic hybrid materials, have shown promise for addressing this challenging task due to their unparalleled features. Herein, recent advances of using MOFs as separating agents for the separation and purification of LHs, including the purification of CH4 , and the separations of alkynes/alkenes, alkanes/alkenes, C5 -C6 -C7 normal/isoalkanes, and C8 alkylaromatics, are summarized. The relationships among the structural and compositional features of the newly synthesized MOF materials and their separation properties and mechanisms are highlighted. Finally, the existing challenges and possible research directions related to the further exploration of porous MOFs in this very active field are also discussed.

The separation and purification of light hydrocarbons (LHs) mixtures is one of the most significantly important but energy demanding processes in the petrochemical industry. As an alternative technology to energy intensive traditional separation methods, such as distillation, absorption, extraction, etc., adsorptive separation using selective solid adsorbents could potentially not only lower energy cost but also offer higher efficiency. The need to develop solid materials for the efficiently selective adsorption of LHs molecules, under mild conditions, is therefore of paramount importance and urgency. Metal– organic frameworks (MOFs), emerging as a relatively new class of porous organic–inorganic hybrid materials, have shown promise for addressing this challenging task due to their unparalleled features. Herein, recent advances of using MOFs as separating agents for the separation and purification of LHs, including the purification of $\mathsf{C H}_{4},$ and the separations of alkynes/alkenes, alkanes/alkenes, $\mathsf{C}_{5}{\mathsf{-C}}_{6}{\mathsf{-C}}_{7}$ normal/isoalkanes, and $c_{8}$ alkylaromatics, are summarized. The relationships among the structural and compositional features of the newly synthesized MOF materials and their separation properties and mechanisms are highlighted. Finally, the existing challenges and possible research directions related to the further exploration of porous MOFs in this very active field are also discussed.
## 1. Introduction
Light hydrocarbons (LHs), namely, $\mathrm{{C_{1}}\mathrm{{-}}\mathrm{{C_{9}}}}$ hydrocarbons, are essential energy resources and feedstocks for the produc­ tion of some industrially important chemicals, which mainly originate from petrochemical or natural gas processing.[1]
The separation and purification of these LHs mixtures into pure species is a very critical and challenging industrial process, and has always been highly concerned by scientists. Typically, distillation, extraction, and absorption are the three main con­ ventional methods of LHs purification in the process of industrial production, but all of them suffer from high energy con­ sumption, great installation cost, and poor economic performance. By contrast, the separation by means of adsorption process utilizing porous solids, such as silica gels, activated alumina, activated carbons, molecular sieve carbons, zeolites, etc., based on both the properties of adsorbent (composition, surface polarity, pore struc­ ture etc.) and separated molecules (mole­ cular size or chemical affinity), has been recognized as a more eco-friendly, recy­ clable, and energy efficient technology.[2–6] However, the properties of solid adsor­ bents manifestly play a key role in this developing technology and largely deter­ mine the final separation efficiency. It is thus very imperative to explore efficient adsorbing materials for the separation and purification of LHs with better energysaving and less adverse impacts.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) (also known as porous coordination polymers), emerging as a new class of crystalline porous materials assembled by inorganic nodes or prefabri­ cated secondary building units (SBUs) and organic linkers, have become one of the hottest topics of research in a wide range of fields of science, such as catalysis,[7–10] luminescence,[11–13] bio­ medicine,[14–16] chemical sensing,[17–19] gas storage,[20–22] proton conduction,[23–25] energy conversion,[26,27] and separation,[28–39] due to their advantageous and unique characteristics including extra-high surface area, structural tenability, linkers tailorability, and controllable properties. Among these application topics, separation has always remained in the mainstream, especially for the separation and purification of LHs, as they offer a myriad of materials design opportunities and the fact that their intrinsic properties have been already shown to be particularly suitable for this challenging task. As a matter of fact, compared to traditional porous materials, such as zeolites and activated carbons, MOF materials possess a number of unique intrinsic features, which make them specifically promising in this regard (Figure 1, left). One of the most appealing features of MOFs rel­ evant to LHs separation and purification is their well-defined pore structures which can be easily adjusted or changed in a large range to allow porous space/channel be more rationally tuned and guarantee a shape/size selectivity toward targeted molecules. Another distinct feature of MOFs, in some cases, is their structural flexibility and dynamic behavior, which enables MOFs exhibit some specific properties such as temperature/ pressure dependent molecular sieving or gate-opening effect when they are stimulated by external stimuli.[40–42] Moreover, MOFs with 3D pore/cage spaces are amenable to embed var­ ious functional groups into their channels and cavities, which is resulting in different binding affinities to adsorbate mole­ cules and allowing a high degree of control over a variety of host–guest interactions. However, these features, which are particularly suitable for the separation and purification of LHs, are relatively difficult and limited to be achieved for activated carbons or zeolite frameworks.
In general, LHs molecules are physically adsorbed inside the pores and interact with the adsorbing sites on the frame­ work of MOFs through van der Waals forces, metal–substrate interactions, and hydrogen bonding interactions.[43,44] Typi­ cally, steric, thermodynamic, kinetic effects, or combinations thereof are present in guest adsorption by MOFs,[31,44–46] and these separation processes can afford molecular separation via diverse microscopic mechanisms: a) Molecular sieving effect: Separation of the certain species from mixed LHs is based on a steric mechanism, and it is an outcome of size/shape exclu­ sion (Figure 2a); b) Thermodynamic equilibrium separation: In this case, the separation performance is mainly decided by the interaction strength (relative affinities) of pore wall of MOFs with the adsorbed LHs molecules, which is generally expressed as a relatively preferential affinity for a given molecule due to its strongly enhanced host–guest interactions[31] (Figure  2b);c) Kinetics driven separation: The LHs molecules are separated by the differential diffusing rates according to their varied actions in the pores of MOFs (Figure 2c); d) Conformation driven sepa­ ration: Separation is achieved via efficiently controlling the guest’s conformation in the cavities of MOF, which is governed by the steric or packing effect instead of host–guest affinity. Namely, the more efficiently packed species are accommodated by the pores/cages of MOFs, and others are not (Figure  2d). e) Framework-flexibility driven separation: The structural transformability of flexible MOFs allows them to undergo “gate opening or breathing effects”-type structural changes in response to external stimuli, just like a saloon door or a balloon that opens or swells when the guest molecules pass, and then the gate or window closes again immediately (Figure 2e).[41,47,48]
In this progress report, we have emphasized the major research progresses recently documented in using MOFs as separating agents for the separation and purification of LHs mixtures, including the purification of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and the sepa­ rations of alkynes/alkenes $(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{-C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}{-}{\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6})$ , alkanes/alkenes $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4}–C_{2}H_{6}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}–C_{3}H_{8}}$ , and $\mathrm{C}_{4,\mathrm{{\olefins}}}{-}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{x})$ , $\mathrm{C}_{5}{-}\mathrm{C}_{6}{-}\mathrm{C}_{7}$ aliphatic normal/isoalkanes, and ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ alkylaromatics (Figure  1). Our focus is to analyze and understand the rela­ tionships among size/shape of pores, framework structures, separating selectivity, and adsorbing capacity of porous MOF adsorbents in these processes, which could provide some useful insights to chemists and material specialists for the fur­ ther design and synthesis of new MOF-based adsorbents for LHs separations. Furthermore, we will comprehensively assess
