## 2. Separation and Purification of $C H_{4}$
$\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is the principal component of natural gas, coal bed gas, biogas, associated gas from crude oil, and refinery gas, which can act as one of the most important energy resources. How­ ever, there are always a variable amount of impurities, such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ , and heavier hydrocarbons, in naturally occur­ ring $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ depending on the source. On the one hand, the presence of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ not only reduces the heating values of these energy-related gases, but can also contribute to climate change and to some extent leads to pipeline corrosion. On the other hand, heavier hydrocarbons including $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , etc., are high value-added petrochemical feedstock. Among them, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ are usually used as the building blocks to synthesis polyethylene-based materials, which are thus uneconomic to be directly used as fuels. To increase the commercial values and combustion efficiency of such energy gases, it is very important to make them meet a certain purity specification.
## 2.1. $\mathsf{C H}_{4}\mathsf{-C O}_{2}$
Compared with $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has a larger polarizability $(29.1^{-}\mathrm{\stackrel{.}{\times}}10^{-25}\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $25.9\times10^{-25}\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ and quadru­ pole moment $(4.30\times10^{-26}$ esu $\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , 0 of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}\mathrm{,}$ ,[33] which, in many cases, is resulting in a stronger interaction between the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and MOFs. As a result, the introduction of polar functional sites $\left(-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ , $-\mathrm{OH}$ , ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ , $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , etc.) $\lvert^{[49-55]}$ and open metals sites $(\mathrm{OMSs})^{[20,34,56-58]}$ into the porous framework can further improve the selective separation capacity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Gener­ ally speaking, most rigid MOFs for the selective removal of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ are belonging to the mechanisms of molecular sieving effect, thermodynamic equilibrium separation, or kinetics driven separation, which are decided by the properties and aperture size/shape of MOFs and the intrinsic nature of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecule.
By taking advantage of the difference in kinetic diameters of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(0.33\ \mathrm{nm})$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(0.38~\mathrm{nm})$ molecules, the separation can be effectively achieved though precisely tuning of the pore size/shape to make $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ diffuse easily in MOFs but block the larger $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molecule. Changing the length of the ligands and constructing interpenetrated MOFs are two general strategies to control over the pore size/shape of MOFs for improving the efficiency of molecular-sieving separation. For example, $\mathrm{{Mn}(H C O_{2})_{2}}$ was one early example that had demonstrated possible molecular sieving separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .[59] It selectively adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but excluded $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with larger kinetic diameters at the given conditions. In a recent study, we had rationally constructed a multi-cage-based MOF (NUM-3) by angle-directed and face-directed mixed-ligands strategy.[60] The activated NUM-3a was capable of separating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with the selectivity of 11.0 (50:50 of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ by the size selectivity. In addition, Zaworotko and co-workers synthesized two supramolecular isomers Qc-5-Cu-dia and Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\cdot\alpha.^{[61]}$ Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\mathbf{\nabla}\beta$ was obtained through an irrevers­ ible structural transformation of Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\alpha$ desolvation, and compared with Qc-5-Cu-dia $(4.8\mathrm{~\AA~})$ and Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\alpha$ (3.8 Å), the resultant Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\beta$ exhibited smaller pore diameters $(3.3\mathring\mathrm{~A})$ . $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ sorption experiments had demonstrated that Qc-5-Cu-sql- $\cdot\beta$ had an ultrahigh selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ( $\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{CM}}=3300_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ ), thanks to such fine tuning of pore-size.
Different from aforementioned molecular sieving separation, some of the rigid MOFs can take advantage of the strong interac­ tions between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and functional groups/sites that immobilized within the host frameworks to facilitate adsorptive separations. In these cases, separations were achieved mainly based on the kinetics driven separation or thermodynamic equilibrium sepa­ ration, namely, the adsorption selectivity of these systems can be affected by both of physical properties of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ including quadruple moment, polarity, etc., and surface properties of the pores of MOFs. For example, MOFs functionalized by OMSs, amines, $-\mathrm{OH}$ group, etc., have been proven to allow for offering higher affinity toward $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ through tuning of the acidity and polarity of the porous environment,[34,54,55,58,62–67] thus, resulting in stronger host–guest interactions to enhance selectivity and adsorption amount for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the presence of other gases. With these points in mind, we had systematically studied the effects of the $\mathcal{-}\mathrm{CONR}(\mathrm{H})_{2}$ ,[54,67,68] $-\mathrm{F}$ , $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ ,[65] pyrazole groups,[66,67] uncoordinated nitrogen atoms,[69] $\mathrm{OMSs}^{[58,66,67,70]}$ on modulating the separating selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and the results obtained from these studies were really promising. Moreover, in our recent studies, we had also constructed a series of microporous MOFs with multiple active sites (i.e., PMOF1 $\{[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}]_{4}[\mathrm{Zn}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{BTC})_{6}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]\cdot3\mathrm{DMA}\}_{n}$ and PMOF2 $\{[\mathrm{NH}_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}][\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{BTC})]\cdot\mathrm{DMA}\}_{n}$ (where $\mathrm{BTC=}$ benzenetricarbo­ xylate and $\mathrm{DMA}=N,N $ dimethylaniline) with OMSs and guest dimethyl­amine cations,[55] LCU-101 $\mathrm{[Fe}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC)(DMAC)}_{3}]_{n}$ and LCU-102 $\{\mathrm{[H_{2}N(C H_{3})_{2}]}$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{Na}_{3}(\mathrm{BPDC})(\mathrm{DMAC})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]\cdot\mathrm{DMAC}\}_{n},$ (where $\mathrm{{H_{4}B P D C}\ =}$ biphenyl- $3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetracarboxylic acid and ${\mathrm{DMAC}}=$ dimethylacetamide) with multiple Fe-cluster subunits and anionic frameworks[58]), for selective separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . As expected, these multiple active sites within MOFs generated a synergistic effect that can greatly enhance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2I}$ framework interactions to facilitate the adsorption selectivity.
Ideally, MOFs with optimizing the pore/cage sizes and spe­ cific functional sites can enable a “sweet spot” of kinetics and thermodynamics. UTSA-16 is an earlier example that illuminated this concept, which displayed extraordinarily high volumetric uptake $(160\ c m^{3}\ c m^{-3})$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at ambient conditions.[63] Neutron diffraction studies demonstrated that the optimal pore cages and the strong interactions between terminal water molecules and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ collaboratively enforced the high storage capacity and selectivity of UTSA-16 for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation. Similarly, Zaworotko and co-workers had further developed the well-known SIFSIX series and addressed how to optimize such a “sweet spot” of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation through crystal engineering or isoreticular chem­ istry.[62] By precisely controlling over pore size of several SIFSIX MOFs, coupled with favorable electrostatic interactions provided by an array of $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anions (Figure 3a), these MOFs afforded a synergistic effect for enhanced separation (Figure  3b,c). Thus, such dual-functionality collaborative results enabled an optimal $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ separation selectivity and capacity at ambient conditions.
In addition, flexible MOFs featuring structural flexibility and dynamic behavior can also perform the separation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ mixture, and generally undergo reversible structural changes in response to external stimuli such as guest inclusion.[41,48] Very recently, Long and co-workers demonstrated that the flexible MOF $\mathrm{Co(bdp)}$ $(\mathrm{bdp}^{2-}=1,4$ -benzenedipyrazolate),[71] which was once well known for its $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage application,[72,73] achieved a near-perfect $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity under 50:50 mixtures of the two gases. In situ powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) experiments indicated that this outstanding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity resulted from reversible guest templating, in which the 1D channels of the collapsed framework expanded to form an aperture ideally sized to admit $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but not $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ and then collapsed to the non­ templated phase upon desorption (Figure 4a–c). As a result, the flexible $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bdp})$ achieves high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity for a wide range of pressures via reversible $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ templating (Figure  4b). However, under a $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ -rich atmosphere (with an equilibrium $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ molar ratio of 6:94), $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{bdp})$ adsorbs minor amounts of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ along with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 4d), indicating the importance of using multicomponent measurements to study all composition ranges and relevant pressure when accurately examining the gas separation performance of a structurally flexible material.
## 2.2. $\mathsf{C H}_{4}\mathsf{-C}_{2-3}\mathsf{H}_{x}$
Separation of $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ hydrocarbons $(\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x},\ x=2,\ 4,\ 6,\ 8$ , namely, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}})$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ into single component is challenging but meaningful and impor­ tant for natural gas upgrading.[30] This is primarily because the recovery of $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ not only can upgrade the quality of natural gas for its further efficient usage, but also can provide other raw chemicals and energy sources of $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}$ for further chemical processing and transformation. In view of the fact that the kinetic diameters of these $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}$ are relatively close and only range from 3.3 to $4.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ,[33] designing micropo­ rous MOFs with pore sizes close to their kinetic diameters is of great significance but challenging for the efficient separation of these small LHs. It is noted that molecular sieving effect and host–guest interactions play important roles in efficiently sepa­ rating their mixtures within a MOF.
In fact, to remove $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}$ mixture is compara­ tively easier because the interactions of $\mathrm{CH_{4}/M O F s}$ are rela­ tively weaker than $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}/\mathrm{MOFs}.^{\mathrm{(\cdot)}}$ 30,74,75] However, as the pair molecules, such as $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}},$ and ${\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}}/{\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}}.$ in a mixture of $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ have comparable physics and chemistry properties, the main difficulty of this purification process is therefore separating such $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixture into individual species. At present, how to accurately control the pore structures of MOFs is still the key to effectively achieving separation of the mixtures, and the methods of framework interpenetration have shown great promise to address this challenging task by molecular sieving effects.[31,76,77] However, it should be pointed out that the methodology of interpenetra­ tion can be used to prepare MOFs with smaller pore sizes, but which can also greatly reduce the specific surface area of MOFs. In addition, enhancing the $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}/\mathrm{MOFs}$ interactions can also help to improve the $\mathrm{C}_{2-3}\mathrm{H}_{x}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation selectivity and capacity. Reported strategies mainly include functionalizing the interior surface, optimizing pore sizes, and especially incorpo­ rating OMSs.[29,30,78–81] Herein it is very necessary to highlight the excellent performance for the separation of 6-componet $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ into each individual component on Fe-MOF-74 due to its high density of OMSs (Figure  5a,b), which was discovered by Long and co-workers in $2012.^{[78]}$ It was found that the Fe-MOF-74 displayed a strong affinity for the unsaturated hydrocarbons $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ (Figure  5c), and the breakthrough experiment showed that Fe-MOF-74 could efficiently distinguish $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture (Figure  5d). Plonka et  al. reported two micropo­ rous calcium-based MOFs Stony Brook MOF (SBMOF-1) and SBMOF-2 with 1D open channels, which could significantly separate $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ hydrocarbon from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ selectivity as high as 74 in SBMOF-1.[80] The authors contributed such a high selectivity to the pore geometry in SBMOF-1, which had provided some of strong adsorption sites through $\mathrm{C-H}\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ interactions.
Moreover, the “flexible” nature of given MOFs can also be used to realize adsorptive separation of LHs. The fine-tuning of structural flexibility has proven to be an important factor in the material design for potential applications of separation of $\mathrm{C}_{1}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}$ gas mixtures. Kitagawa and co-workers synthesized two flexible 2D MOFs CID-5 and CID-6, and their solid solutions $\mathrm{CID-}5/6.^{[74]}$ The authors found that the framework flexibility of CID-5/6 can be precisely adjusted by changing the ligand-based solution compounds, and thus to optimize the separation effi­ ciencies. Two-component mixed gas breakthrough experiments demonstrated that CID-5/6 could effectively separate $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Zhou and co-workers first designed and synthesized a mesh-adjustable molecular sieve (MAMS), based on MOF’s dynamic porous properties.[81] The MOF-based MAMS has a temperature-induced molecular-gating effect, namely, with the increase of temperature, the gates of MAMS-1–4 open linearly, thus resulting in a temperature-controlled molecular sieve, and the sizes of the gates can in principle be adjusted to separate any two gases with kinetic diameters in the range of $2.9\mathrm{-}\bar{5}.05\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Importantly, both of MAMS-1 and MAMS-2 could significantly separate binary $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4},$ $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{4}H_{10}}$ at 143, 195, and $231~\mathrm{K}$ respectively.
## 3. Separation of Alkynes from Alkenes
Efficient separation of alkynes/alkenes mixtures, such as acety­ lene $\left(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ and ethylene $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4})}$ , propyne $\mathrm{(C_{3}H_{4})}$ , and propylene $\mathrm{(C_{3}H_{6})}$ , is one of most crucial and important industrial processes, but it is also full of challenges due to their very similar physical– chemical properties. MOFs, due to their unique properties, have been demonstrated to be promising adsorbents to traditional nanoporous materials for the separation of alkynes from alkenes. During the last decade, intense research efforts have been devoted to exploiting the excellent MOF materials for this task.[82–90]
## 3.1. ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{-}{\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$
Both $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are important starting materials in the petrochemical and electronic industry. In chemical industry, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ is mainly produced from steam cracking of naphtha and ethane, which generally coexist with a small amount of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (about $1\%$ as a side product. The presence of this trace $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ stream not only can dramatically impact the quality of the resulting polyethylene during $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ polymerization, but it also can form solid metal acetylides and further block the fluid stream. Thus, it is imperative that $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the ethylene feed should be reduced to an acceptable level.
The first exploration of MOFs for highly selective separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ was performed by Chen and co-workers on two isostructural 3D mixed-MOFs (M’MOFs), namely M’MOF-2 and M’MOF-3.[87] The activated M’MOF-3a has smaller pore sizes than M’MOF-2a because of the more flexible organic ligand. M’MOF-3a can significantly differentiate $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ with a high selectivity of 25.5 at $195\mathrm{~K~}$ while M’MOF-2a displayed a rel­ atively low of 1.6. The authors attributed such an interesting phe­ nomenon to the smaller micropore within M’MOF-3a, in which $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule was allowed to enter into its pore, while $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ was blocked or experienced slow kinetics. This work suggested the importance of kinetically controlled framework flexibility and size-exclusion effect for practical adsorption-based separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ .
In a practical application, the ideal MOF materials for sepa­ ration should not only exhibit high adsorption selectivity but also optimal uptake capacity and kinetics for the given species. In other words, a major barrier of “trade-off” should be over­ come for a microporous solid adsorbent during the gas sepa­ ration and purification. For instance, as is mentioned above, the M’MOF families, especially for M’MOF-3a, have extraordi­ narily high sieving effects that enable high separation selectivi­ ties, but their ultra-micropores have also limited the uptake of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .[87,91] The MOF-74 series possess high densities of OMSs that dramatically drive high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake, but their pores are too large to introduce size-sieving effects and thus resulted in a lower selectivity for the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture.[30,78]
For this, to improve adsorption selectivity but without sacri­ ficing uptake capacity, we have developed a unique microporous MOF UTSA-100 with dual-functionalized sites for the efficient separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures.[86] The activated UTSA100a has narrow opening windows of $3.3\mathring\mathrm{~A}$ and suitable pore/ cage diameter of $4.{\overset{\textstyle-}{0}}\{\overset{\textstyle\cdot}{\mathrm{A}}$ (Figure 6a), which preferentially took up much more $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (a selectivity of 10.72), while the weak acid–base interactions between $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules further enforced its high adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Figure  6b,c). Such a balanced reasonably high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity and separation selectivity make UTSA-100a possess the superior separation performance than other well-known MOFs, such as M’MOF-3a,[87] Fe-MOF-74, Co-MOF-74, Mg-MOF-74,[78] and NOTT-300,[83] leading to its highly efficient $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ sep­ aration efficiency (Figure 6d; Table S1, Supporting Information).
Another important study in this issue was carried out by Cui et  al. in 2016.[82] To overcome this trade-off problem, they have designed a series of $(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})^{2-}$ -pillared square-grid-structured MOFs materials (called SIFSIX, Figure 7a), which were also pre­ viously well known for their outstanding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption prop­ erties.[62] The pore/window sizes of SIFSIX compounds can be well controlled by creating an interpenetrated polymorph struc­ ture or changing the length of the organic linker. On the one hand, the specific identification and high adsorption capacity (2.1 millimoles per gram at 0.025 bar) of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules on SIFSIX are achieved by optimizing hydrogen-bond interactions between the weakly acidic $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and strong basic $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ sites, and van der Waals interactions between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and the organic linkers. On the other hand, the spatial geometric distribution of the anion and the pore sizes of MOFs is regulated to achieve a synergistic effect between the host–guest and/or guest–guest and thus obtain an extremely high adsorption selectivity (39.7–44.8) for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at ambient conditions. Transient breakthrough simulations demonstrated that $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ first eluted through the bed to yield a polymer-grade gas (Figure 7b), and the dimensionless $\tau_{\mathrm{break}}$ values for SIFSIX-1-Cu and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i exceed those of the other SIFSIX materials and MOFs (Figure  7c,d). Fol­ lowing this study, Li et al.[85] further elaborately designed a novel SIFSIX material SIFSIX-14-Cu-i (also termed as UTSA-200a) with ultrafine 1D channel $(3.4\mathrm{~\AA~})$ that is comparable in size to $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule $(3.3\mathrm{~\AA~})$ to effectively block $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ molecule $(4.2\mathrm{~\AA~})$ . Importantly, UTSA-200 retains the strong binding sites for the record-high $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake capacity $(58~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , under $298\mathrm{K}$ and 0.01 bar). The breakthrough experiments demon­ strated that UTSA-200a was capable of removing of trace $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixture $(1/99)$ to produce $99.9999\%$ pure $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ with 1.18 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity. These two works not only offer a solution for the efficient and energysaving separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures, but also provide some guidance to facilitate the design of other ideal adsorbing and separating materials for LHs separation and purification.
## 3.2. $\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{-C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{6}$
$\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ is the second most important olefin raw material in the world (120 million tons of global production in 2016),[92] which is also produced primarily by steam cracking of hydrocarbons or petroleum fractions, and therefore inevitably contains trace amount of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ component. In practical production, in order to meet the requirement of polymer-grade $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ , the content of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ must be reduced to $5\mathrm{ppm}$ or even less than 1 ppm. How­ ever, capture and removal of such a trace $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ impurity from $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ remains a very challenging task. It is not only because the structures and molecular sizes $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{3}H_{4}}$ : $4.16\times4.01\times6.51\mathrm{~\AA~}^{3}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ : $5.25\times4.16\times6.44\mathrm{~\AA^{3})^{[93]}}$ of these two molecules are very similar, but also due to the ultralow content of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ in the mixtures.
Using MOF as an adsorbing material to separate $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixtures is still in its infancy, and only a few publications had been reported to date.[88–90] The first example was realized by Li et  al. on a flexible-robust MOF $\mathrm{[Cu}(4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine)(trifluo­ romethanesulfonate $\left(\mathrm{OTf}\right)^{-}_{2}]$ (ELM-12) in 2017.[90] (ELM-12 showed very high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ adsorption selectivity (ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixture $(1/99,\mathrm{V/V})$ was estimated to be 84) and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ adsorp­ tion capacity in the low pressure range. Neutron powder dif­ fraction studies and first-principles dispersion-corrected den­ sity functional theory (DFT) calculations had confirmed that the high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ adsorption selectivity originated from the strong binding of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ with the polar OTf− groups and the unique dumbbell-shaped pores in ELM-12 for the sieving effect. Importantly, in actual breakthrough experiments (1/99, V/V of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixture), the MOF successfully removed the trace $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ to a concentration below 2  ppm and thus obtained an ultrahigh purity of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ $(>99.9998\%)$ . This work has initiated the promise of flexible-robust MOFs for the important hydrocarbon separation and purification processes.
Recently, Xing and co-workers first reported an efficient strategy of “single-molecule $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ trap adsorption” for the highly efficient removal of trace $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ from $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ .[89] Based on the shape characteristic of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ molecule, the authors constructed a family of suitable anion-pillared hybrid ultramicroporous materials (including SIFSIX-1-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, SIFSIX-3-Ni, SIFSIX-3-Zn, and NBOFFIVE-1-Ni) to enhance the “specificity” and “affinity” of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ molecule. Interestingly, the pore space and geometric disposition of $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ anion could be precisely varied in $0.1\mathrm{-}0.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ scale for the best match of the size/shape/interaction sites of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ molecules, which made these pyrazine-based MOFs realize the single-molecule $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ adsorption (Figure  8a). This single-molecule $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ trap thus enabled extremely high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ uptake capacity $(2.65\mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}})$ + at an ultralow concentration $(3000~\mathrm{ppm})$ (Figure  8b), as well as an high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ (1/99, V/V) selectivity $(>250)$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure  8c). However, their pore sizes allow both $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ to enter, thus limiting the productivity of the desired $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ product to a certain extent. To realize high productivity and purity simultaneously (so-called trade-off), Chen and co-workers selected 20 different MOFs to screen them with broad types of structures, pore sizes, functionalities, and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ adsorption properties.[93] Among them, UTSA-200 exhibits an unprec­ edented high $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ uptake $(95\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at 0.01 bar and 298 K) and record $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ selectivity, setting new benchmarks for the removal of ultralow concentration $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ from 1:99 and 0.1:99.9 (v/v) $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ mixtures for any material reported so far. The authors attributed such an exceptional separation per­ formance to the suitable dynamic pore size originated from the rotation of pyridine rings inside the pores to efficiently block the larger $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ molecule, while the exposed $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}{}^{2-}$ group in the pores can form a strong binding site to capture smaller $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}}$ .
## 4. Separation of Alkanes from Alkenes
Light olefins, such $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ , 1,3-butadiene $\left(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\right)$ , $n$ -butene $(n\mathrm{-C_{4}H_{8}})$ , and isobutene $(\mathrm{i}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8})$ , are distinctly essential building blocks for manufacturing plastics. Among them, global production of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ nearly exceeds 200 million tonnes per year.[94] In the chemical industry, alkenes are usually produced by hydrocarbon cracking and dehydrogenation of the corresponding alkanes, and as a result some alkane byproducts are inevitably generated. MOFs materials have demonstrated great potential and impressive alkane/alkene separation abilities, which were mainly achieved by one or more of the following strategies: 1) size/shape exclusion; 2) kinetic driven effect; 3) host–guest interactions; and 4) gate-opening effect.
## 4.1. $C_{2}H_{4}-C_{2}H_{6}$
MOFs with exposed cation sites, such as OMSs, have strong interactions with unsaturated hydrocarbons molecules,[78,95] which has been proven to be one of the most efficient options for the $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation.[29,31,45] Isostructural MMOF-74 materials $[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Co}$ , Mn, Ni, Mg, Fe, Zn, etc.) with high-density OMSs (7.13–7.58 mmol $\mathrm{M}^{2+}$ per $\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ ) were performed exception­ ally well for the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures.[29,45,78,96–98] Mg-MOF-74 was the first MMOF-74 for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separa­ tion.[97] This MOF showed similar saturation capacities for $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ at a given temperature, but Henry’s constant and zero coverage isosteric heat of adsorption $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ are much higher than those of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6},$ revealing a stronger interaction between the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecules and specific sites in Mg-MOF-74 framework by the $\pi$ -complexation. Among these MMOF-74 materials, Fe-MOF-74 had been demonstrated to perform even better for separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture due to the soft, high-spin $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ coordination sites exposed on its surface.[78] For an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture at 318 K, 0.005–1.00  bar, the adsorption selectivities obtained for Fe-MOF-74 of 13 to 18 were significantly greater than either the isostructural Mg-MOF-74 of 4 to 7 or zeolite NaX of 9 to 14.[97,99] Neutron powder diffraction experiments confirmed that coordinatively unsaturated $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ cations were the strongest adsorption sites, and ethylene was adsorbed indeed via the anticipated side-on binding modes with a Fe–C distance of 2.42(2) Å. In a typical breakthrough experiment, Fe-MOF-74 could separate an equi­ molar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture into $99\%$ and $99.5\%$ pure compo­ nents at 1 bar and $318\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively.
In addition, for the family of MOF-74, herein it is very nec­ essary to highlight the excellent performance for the separa­ tion of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ on a functionalized Fe-MOF-74, which was reported by Chen and co-workers recently.[100] In this work, the authors used oxygen molecules to combine with the unsatu­ rated vacancies in the Fe-MOF-74 to form Fe-peroxo sites, which effectively block the $\pi$ bond interaction between the unsaturated metal vacancies and $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (Figure  9a), and thus significantly reducing the $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ adsorption. Meanwhile, the newly constructed material $\displaystyle(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})(\mathrm{dobdc}))$ ) exhibited stronger adsorption affinity with $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ over ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ (Figure  9b) and thus highly selective separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (Figure  9c,d). More importantly, this material can straightforwardly produce poly­ mer-grade $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $(\geq99.99\%$ pure) from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures during the first adsorption cycle with moderately high produc­ tivity and a low energy cost (Figure  9d,e). This work not only subtly achieved the “reversed $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ adsorption,” but also provided a broadly applicable strategy immobilizing different sites into porous MOFs for the preferential binding of $\mathrm{cC}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ .
Another well-known MOF with leading $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separa­ tion performance based on host–guest interactions is NOTT300, which exhibited the highest IAST $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity (48.7) reported so far.[83] The combined DFT calculation and ine­ lastic neutron scattering spectra indicated that ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ was aligned at a very long distance to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group $(\mathsf{C}\cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot\mathsf{O}=5.07\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) due to the lack of $\pi$ electron density and repulsion between the H atoms, and thus resulted in a very weak interaction with the NOTT-300 host. While the $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ stacking between $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ mole­ cules as well as the overlap between $\pi$ electrons of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ and the $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups from the framework yielded the stronger binding of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ to NOTT-300, which also accounted for the selective adsorption of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ . Later, Zhang and co-workers reported a zinc azolate framework MAF-49, which could selectively trap $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ to produce highly pure $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ $(99.95\%+)$ from $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixtures.[101] The grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations and further periodic DFT calculations had revealed that such a high selectively might attribute to the appropriate positioning of multiple dipole repulsion groups and hydrogen-bonding acceptors on the ultramicroporous pore surface, which allowed multiple ${\mathrm{C-H}}\cdot\cdot\cdot{\mathrm{N}}$ hydrogen attractive interactions with $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}$ instead of the more polar competitor $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ .
Apart from constructing MOFs with OMSs or functional groups to perform thermodynamic equilibrium separation, another general guideline is to fine-tune small aperture sizes in MOF materials for the separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture via molecular-sieving or gate-opening effect. A typical example is ZIF-7 that illustrated this concept mainly.[102,103] The adsorp­ tion isotherms of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ on the ZIF-7 framework at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ were Type IV, which could be distinguished into three regions: a region where the uptake was very negligible at low pressure; a step region where the uptake rapidly increased when a “gate-opening” event occurred; and a region after which the uptake developed more slowly with the pressure.
The authors attributed these three regions to adsorption at the external surface, gate opening, and adsorption inside the cavities, and further filling of the cavities with adsorbate, respectively. Accordingly, in a breakthrough experiment, ZIF-7 could efficiently separate ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ from an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture.[102] Since the triple symmetry of the methyl group of ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ is more suitable for the largest opening of the ZIF-7 cage, it can advantageously interact with the triangular window of the framework, and thus ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ can penetrate at lower pres­ sures than $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ . Similarly, ZIF-8,[104–106] PCN-245,[107] and PCN-250,[108] as reported recently, have also been reported for selectively adsorbing $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ over $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ based on molecularsieving or gate-opening effect.
More recently, Chen et al. report an ultramicroporous MOF $\mathrm{[Ca(C_{4}O_{4})(H_{2}O)]}$ (termed UTSA-280),[109] synthesized from calcium nitrate and squaric acid, that possesses rigid 1D chan­ nels with a pore size of $3.2\times4.5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ and $3.8\times3.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ (Figure 10a). These pores show similar cross-sectional areas of ${\approx}14.4\mathrm{~\AA^{2}}$ which are larger than the minimum cross-sectional area of ethylene $(13.7\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2})$ but smaller than that of ethane $(15.5\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2})$ , demonstrating the potential for size/shape separation of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}/$ ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ (Figure 10e). Based on the measured isotherms, it can be calculated that UTSA-280 has an $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ selectivity higher than 10  000, which is orders of magnitude larger than those of Fe-MOF-74 $(13.6)^{[78]}$ and NOTT-300 $(48.7)^{[83]}$ (Figure  10f; Table S2, Supporting Information). As expected, the break­ through experiments further conformed that UTSA-280 can efficiently achieve a challenging $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ mixture separa­ tion with high ethylene productivity under ambient conditions (Figure $\left.10\mathrm{g}\right)$ ). The ethane is first eluted through the bed and the outlet gas is rapidly brought to high purity. Furthermore, singlecrystal X-ray diffraction experiments revealed that the adsorbed ${\mathrm{C}}_{2}{\mathrm{H}}_{4}$ molecules were successfully located in the middle of the channel and the framework of $\mathrm{[Ca(C_{4}O_{4})(H_{2}O)]\cdot0.2C_{2}H_{4}}$ is basically not changed (Figure 10b–d).
## 4.2. $C_{3}H_{6}-C_{3}H_{8}$
Analogous to $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separation, MOFs with a high density of OMSs also can be used as one of most selective materials to separate $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures. In general, these materials are effective in separating both $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ , for instance, the family of MMOF-74,[30,78,96,97,110,111] HKUST-1,[29] NOTT-300,[83] etc. In this respect, HKUST-1 was among the ear­ liest MOFs that had been studied for the separation of these two gases mixtures.[112–114] Typically, as mentioned above, this material presents OMSs of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ as Lewis acid sites, thus, which can act as an electron acceptor to form $\pi$ -bond with the double bond of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ . Comparison of experimental isotherms and GCMC simulations revealed that $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ interacted strongly with $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites, while ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ adsorption occurred preferentially in small octahedral pockets.[114] The binary breakthrough exper­ iments further verified that the separation factors of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ over $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ were ranging from 3.3 (at 313 K) to 5.5 (at 353 K).[115] Moreover, researchers also discovered that the morphologies of HKUST-1 material had a great effect on their separation per­ formance, and the $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ capacity was in the following orders: sphere > tablet > extrudate.[113,115,116]
Like their performance in $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ separations, MMOF-74 series is another attractive material that preferen­ tially binds $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ over $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ at a wide pressure range because of their rich OMSs.[30,78,110] Randall and co-workers systematically studied the separating performance of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ on a series of isostructural MMOF-74 materials $\mathrm{\DeltaM=Co}$ , Mn, and Mg).[110] Experiments and calculations had revealed that, compared with Mn-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74, Co-MOF-74 exhibited the highest thermodynamic equimolar $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ selectivity $_{(\approx46)}$ due to the strong $\pi$ -complexation between ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}$ molecules and open ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites. At about the same time, Long and co-workers demonstrated the excellent performance of Fe-MOF-74 for sep­ aration of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture.[78] Breakthrough experiments on an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture showed that Fe-MOF-74 was capable of separating these two gases into the pure compo­ nent of $99.5\%$ $(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6})$ and $99\%$ $(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8})$ purity at 1 bar and $318\mathrm{K}$
Similarly to other LHs separation, the pore aperture size/shape of MOFs also plays an important role in enhancing the separating selectivity of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixtures. ZIF-8 exhibited a selective adsorption $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ behavior via a molecular sieving mecha­ nism.[106,117] However, the full extraction of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ from $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ by using a partial size-shape sieving-based separation process was a very challenging task due to their close structural dimensions and difficulties in ultrafine tuning of the pore aperture size in $0.2–1$ Å scale. Recently, Eddaoudi and co-workers designed and synthe­ sized a fluorinated 3D MOF NbOFFIVE-1-Ni (also known as KAUST-7) that selectively adsorbed $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ , with the complete exclusion of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ .[118] This MOF was constructed by bridging $\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}$ -pyrazine square-grid layers with $(\mathrm{NbOF}_{5})^{2-}$ pillars (Figure  11a). By judiciously changing the shape, structure, and properties of anionic pillars, the free-rotating pyrazine moieties can be effectively hindered, and thus delimiting the final pore size and its maximum opening. Compared to its isostructural MOF (SIFSIX-3-Ni),[82,84] the important feature of this MOF is that the pillaring inorganic building block $(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})^{2-}$ was replaced by a larger anionic ligand $(\mathrm{NbOF}_{5})^{2-}$ (Figure  11a). As a result, this change shortened the distance between two $(\mathrm{NbOF}_{5})^{2-}$ building blocks from the two neighboring layers and induced a tilting of pyrazine mole­cules, and consequently decreased the pore aperture size, comparatively with that of SIFSIX-3-Ni. Meanwhile, the pore size of KAUST-7 could be effectively reduced from 4.965 to $\mathbf{\bar{3.047\:\mathring{A}}}$ , and the maximum opening size was also reduced from 7.314 to $4.752\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ Such a restricted aperture size opening permitted smaller $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ molecules to transport into and out of the pore system, while excluded the slightly bulkier $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ , and thus KAUST-7 exhibited essentially infinite $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ selectivity (Figure 11b,c). This study shows the unique advantage of finely regulated MOF materials in the separation of gases (especially similar gases) and provides an important reference for future research and industrial application.
In addition, Li and co-workers recently reported a micropo­ rous MOF, $\mathrm{Y}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{abtc})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}(\mathrm{DMA})_{2}$ (Y-abtc, abtc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -azobenzene-tetracarboxylates; DMA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ dimethylam monium), with cage-like pores, which was constructed by a strategy of topology-guided SBU replacement.[119] The pore structure of Y-abtc was precisely tuned by judicious selection of structure topology, organic linkers, and inorganic nodes for the separation of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture. This tailor-made Y-abtc is both hydrothermally and thermally robust, and adsorbs $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ with fast kinetics but fully excludes ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , as a result of selective size exclusion. Meanwhile, the multicomponent breakthrough experiments confirmed that polymer-grade propylene $(99.5\%)$ can be obtained, meeting the “polymer grade” specification required for the production of polypropylene.
## 4.3. Separating $\mathsf{\pmb{C}}_{4,\mathrm{\pmb{\Lambda}}}$ olefins from $\pmb{\mathsf{C}}_{4}\mathsf{H}_{x}$ Mixtures
$\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins including $^{1,3-C_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}}$ , ${n\mathrm{-C_{4}H_{8}}}$ , and $\mathrm{i.C_{4}H_{8}}$ are very important feedstock for the production of synthetic rubbers or other chemical products. Typically, $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins are produced by the fluidized catalytic cracking, steam cracking, and methanolto-olefins processes,[2] but the $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins obtained from these techniques are generally consisting $30\mathrm{-}60\%$ $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ , $10{-}20\%$ $n{\mathrm{-C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}$ , $10-30\%$ $\mathrm{i.C_{4}H_{8}}$ , and $3-10\%$ butane $(\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{10})$ .[120] How­ ever, owing to the existing multiple structural and cis/trans isomers, compared with $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ hydrocarbons separation, it is even more difficult to separate these $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ fractions.
Also, MOFs with OMSs, such as HKUST-1,[121,122] ZJNU80,[123] Fe-MOF-74,[124] etc., exhibited high adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins due to the strong interactions between unsaturated hydrocarbons and OMSs via the well-known $\pi$ -complexation. However, these MOFs generally have low or even negligible selectivity owing to elevated shape/sizes similarity and struc­ tural complexity of these $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ fractions. Clearly, it can be seen that individual ${\mathrm{C}_{4}}{\mathrm{H}_{x}}/{\mathrm{MOF}}$ s interactions or size/shape exclu­ sion often met with limited success, and thus dual-functional or multifunctional adsorbents should be exploited to realize highly efficient separation of $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins. With this in mind, Kitagawa and co-workers tried to integrate size-sieving and gate opening effects into a single framework.[125] $\mathrm{In}2016$ , they reported the first example of using a triply interpenetrated flexible MOF SD-65, as an adsorbing material to separate $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ from six major $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbons (cis-2-butene, trans-2-butene, $n$ -butane, 1-butene, isobutene, isobutane) under ambient conditions.
The adsorption isotherms, in situ PXRD, and solid state nuclear magnetic resonance studies of seven $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbons indicated that the larger quadrupole moment of $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ exclusively induced a structural transformation of SD-65 (a gate opening effect). In this way, the overall structure of SD-65 was expanded so that $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ can diffuse into the isolated, small voids and accommo­ date there, and thereby achieving an excellent separation effi­ ciency. This work indicated that the integration of narrow voids and structural flexibility is a powerful strategy for the separa­ tion of $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ mixtures.
In a following study, Xing and co-workers reported another series of dual-functional MOF materials that can efficiently and selectively separate $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins from $\mathrm{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{x}$ by combining molecular recognition and size-sieving.[126] The authors syn­ thesized three new anion-pillared interpenetrated hybrid ultra-microporous MOFs, GeFSIX-2-Cu-i, NbFSIX-2-Cu-i, and GeFSIX-14-Cu-i, for which the functional sites position and pore shape/size can be ultrafinely adjusted in $0.2\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ scale increments via altering the organic linkers and anion pillars to achieve targeted molecular recognition. Through controlling the rotation of the organic linkers, on the other hand, a flex­ ible contracted pore window can be created to enable molec­ ular sieving effect. The actual experimental breakthrough tests confirmed that SIFSIX-2-Cu-i and GeFSIX-2-Cu-i could successfully separate ternary olefin mixture in the sequence of (breakthrough time) ${\mathrm{C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}>n{\cdot}{\mathrm{C}}_{4}{\mathrm{H}}_{8}>$ iso- ${.\mathrm{C_{4}H_{8}}}$ , and binary mixture $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}/n{\cdot}\mathrm{C_{4}H_{8}},$ , $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}/i s o{-}C_{4}H_{8}}$ , and $\boldsymbol{n}{\cdot}\mathsf{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8}/\mathrm{i}\mathrm{s}_{0}{\cdot}\mathsf{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ with high capacities.
In addition, it is very necessary to highlight a guest confor­ mation-controlling MOF material $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTM that could efficiently purified $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ in an optimum adsorption selectivity sequence: $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}<\mathrm{C_{4}H_{10}}<n{\cdot}\mathrm{C_{4}H_{8}}<\mathrm{i}{\cdot}\mathrm{C_{4}H_{8}}$ , reported by Zhang and coworkers recently.[120] The authors proposed using quasidiscrete cavities to limit the cis conformation of flexible guest molecules in higher energy, and used the difference in energy consump­ tion of guest configuration changes to weaken the adsorption of butadiene relative to other $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbons (Figure  12a,b). Thus, the adsorption selectivity opposite to that of conven­ tional porous materials was obtained. In this contribution, they selected $10~\mathrm{MOF}$ materials with distinguished properties for comparison including four OMSs-functionalized MOFs, four hydrophilic MOFs, and two hydrophobic MOFs. Fixed bed breakthrough experiments demonstrated that the separation performances of these OMSs-functionalized or hydrophobic MOFs were not very good, which was mainly because these $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbon molecules have similar polarizabilities and polari­ ties, and each of them can only coordinate with one OMSs. Moreover, the other four typical hydrophilic MOFs represented the large channel (Zr-BDC, where $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}=1,4$ -benzenedicar­ boxylic acid), small channel $(\mathrm{Cu}{\cdot}\mathrm{SiF}_{6})$ , discrete pore (Zn-BTA $(\mathsf{Z n}_{2}(\mathsf{b t a})_{2}$ , where $\mathrm{~\cal~H~}_{2}\mathrm{bta~\Lambda~}=$ bis(5-amino-1H1,2,4-triazol-3-yl) methane)) and quasidiscrete pore (Zn-BTM $(\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{btm})_{2}$ , where $\mathrm{~{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{btm}}=$ bis(5-methyl-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-yl)methane)) situa­ tions. It turned out that $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTM exhibited the best separation performance due to its special ability of controlling guest con­ formation (Figure  12a,d). At room temperature and pressure, when the $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ hydrocarbons mixture flowed through a fixed bed adsorption unit using $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTM as the filler, the $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}}$ mol­ ecules first flowed out, followed by $\mathrm{C_{4}H_{10}}$ , ${n\mathrm{-}C_{4}H_{8}}$ , and $\mathrm{i.C_{4}H_{8}}$ (Figure  12c). Therefore, 1,3-butadiene can be easily and effi­ ciently purified $(\geq99.5\%)$ , and avoids polymerization which may occur in a high temperature environment, and can also effi­ ciently separate the four components.
## 5. Separation of Normal/Isoalkanes
The separation of linear alkanes from their branched isomers is a key task in petroleum refining to enhance octane rating in gasoline. The dibranched $\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{7}$ paraffins are major compo­ nents in high-octane gasoline, which have the relatively higher research octane number (RON) values than their normal coun­ terparts.[36,127,128] In order to produce premium grade gasoline, it is necessary to exclude these $n$ -alkanes from their branched isomers. To date, the related works through adsorption sepa­ rations by using MOF materials are relatively rare, but these efforts have already achieved some great results.[29,36,127–134]
Owing to the chemically inert and similar polarizabilities of these $\mathrm{C}_{5}–\mathrm{C}_{7}$ alkane isomers, there is just leaving shape as a main handle available for their discrimination. However, it has been extremely difficult to meet the stringent require­ ment on the matching kinetic diameter/shape of the adsorbate and pore aperture of the adsorbent. Therefore, it is important to develop efficient strategies that precisely and subtly control the pore structures within MOFs. Chen et al. reported the first example of using MOF, namely, MOF-508, as the gas chroma­ tography stationary phase to separate ${\mathrm{C}}_{5}$ and $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ alkanes.[129] The unique features of MOF-508 were its controllable 1D chan­ nels of $4.08\times4.09\mathrm{~\AA}^{2}$ present in the twofold interpenetrated 3D framework, which selectively accommodated linear alkanes and discriminated branched alkanes. The high level of separating performance was credited to their different van der Waals inter­ actions arising from the subtle matching of the size and shape of the alkanes with the MOF-508 micropores and the concerned vapor sorbates’ kinetic diameters. $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}$ $(\mathrm{BDP}^{2-}=1,4$ -ben­ zenedipyrazolate) with sharply angled crevices running along the triangular channels (Figure  13a,c) was reported by Long and co-workers,[128] which was another important MOF that can efficiently discriminate among the differently shaped ${\mathrm{C}}_{6}$ alkane isomers more finely than the commercial standard. Combining with unary sorption isotherms, configurational-bias Monte Carlo (CBMC) simulations demonstrated the fact that the 1D triangular channels of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}$ with pore width of $4.9\mathring\mathrm{\textrm{A}}$ were not only large enough to readily accommodate all hexane iso­ mers but also could effectively interact with these $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ alkanes, which decreased with the degree of branching (Figure  13d,e). The branched isomers had weaker interactions with the frame­ work than linear $n$ -hexane because they were not flexible enough to maximize van der Waals interactions with the pore surfaces. The breakthrough experiments further confirmed that $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDP})_{3}$ was capable of fractionating an equimolar mixture of hexanes into three modules (mono/dibranched isomers and $n$ -hexane) by the degree of branching, namely, eluted from the bed in the sequence of 2,2-dimethylbutane $>2,2$ -dimethylbu­ tane $>2$ -methylpentane $>3$ -methylpentane >linear $n$ -hexane (Figure  13f,g). Similarly, Eddaoudi and co-workers judiciously designed a pair of new rare-earth metal fcu-MOFs through an isoreticular chemistry approach.[130] The constructed fcu-MOFs consist of two sorts of cages, namely, octahedral and tetrahe­ dral in shape, that were solely interconnected with a triangular window aperture size of about $\mathrm{4.7\mathring{A}}$ , which could act as a sorbatesize cut-off and enable a complete sieving of ${\mathrm{C}}_{5}$ alkanes mixtures.
In addition, on the basis of reticular chemistry, judiciously selecting the organic linkers and inorganic clusters is one of the most basically and effectively designer strategies to con­ struct various structure types of MOFs for a size exclusion or kinetically controlled separation. Guided by topological analysis of the influence of ligand size and geometry (aspect ratio) on the SBU connectivity and resulting framework structure, very recently, Li and co-workers synthesized three $Z\mathrm{r}$ based MOFs through deliberately selected isophthalate based tetratopic linkers with appropriate molecular dimensions.[127] In this way, the distance between adjacent SBUs was reduced and consequently the pore aperture could be also tuned. Among the resulted MOFs, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{bptc})_{3}$ ( ${\tt b p t c}=3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -biphe­ nyltetracarboxylate) with optimized pore structure exhibited an exceptionally high selectivity and adsorption capacity for the separation of $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ alkane isomers, which, in some aspects, out­ performed the benchmark adsorbent, zeolite-5A. And $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{H})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{abtc})_{2}$ (abtc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\ 3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}\mathrm{{;}}$ azobenzene-tetracarboxylate) with channel-like pores was capable of selectively separating all three $\mathrm{C}_{6}$ isomers and possessed an exceptionally high mono/ dibranched separation factor that exceeded the best performing MOFs reported to date.
In fact, individual molecular sieving mechanism often met with limited success, especially considering their multicompo­ nents and complex structures in the mixtures. If combining structural flexibility with size/shape exclusion or other sepa­ rating mechanism into one MOF, some unexpected separation performance may be yielded. As in the example of separation of hexane isomers on flexible $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Fe})(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}$ , a bulky ${\mathrm{-CF}}_{3}$ group was grafted on the terephthalate linker to rationally design the pore size/shape.[134] The result of this integration of the functionalized microporous and structural flexibility facili­ tated such a kinetically controlled molecular sieve, and thus made $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}53(\mathrm{Fe})(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}$ be capable of completely sieving the dibranched hexane isomers from the other components. Simi­ larly, a Ca-based robust microporous MOF, $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ (tcpb $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 1,2,4,5-tetrakis(4carboxyphenyl)-benzene) with 1D channel of $5.5{-}6.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , was also reported by Li’s group lately.[133] Due to its framework flexibility and adsorbate-dependent structure, $\mathrm{Ca(H_{2}t c p b)}$ , by a single adsorbent for the first time, separated a ternary mixture of linear, monobranched, and dibranched alkane isomers into chemically pure individual components through temperature programming.
## 6. Separation of $\mathsf{C}_{8}$ Alkylaromatic Isomers
Separation of benzene derivatives from each other, especially for ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ alkylaromatic isomers, has been believed to be one of seven world-changing separations.[94] In the petrochemical industries, xylene isomers including o-xylene (OX), m-xylene (MX), $p$ -xylene (PX), and ethylbenzene (EB) are indispensable raw materials.[3] So it is of vital importance to efficiently sepa­ rate these xylene isomers into their individual pure components in industrial processes. Owing to their similar physicochemical properties, however, there is a challenge in separating these isomers completely.
## 6.1. Selective Adsorption Based on Packing Effects
Early work on MOFs hinted that the stacking or packing dif­ ferences of $\mathrm{C}_{8}$ alkylaromatics within the MOF channels can be exploited to separate their mixtures.[3,135–140] De Vos and co-workers performed a pioneer work of the separation of xylene isomers on MIL-47(V), MIL-53(Al), and $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ in the liquid phase.[135] Although MIL-47(V) exhibited the highest separation selectivity of PX/MX among these materials, it failed to discriminate between OX and PX. Actually, the subsequent investigations had proven that MIL-47(V) was an orthoselective adsorbing material both in liquid and vapor phases.[136,137,141,142] In a gas-phase adsorption study, the quaternary breakthrough experiment showed that the four types of ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ alkylaromatics could be separated from each other following a trend of $\mathrm{EB}>$ $\mathrm{MX}>\mathrm{PX}>\mathrm{OX}$ at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It was also found that the adsorption selectivity was pressure dependent, namely, increased with an increasing partial pressure or degree of pore filling. Meanwhile, the efficiently separating efficiency of MIL-47(V) at a high pore filling was attributed to the differences in packing modes of these molecules in the pores of MIL-47.
Dubbeldam and co-workers had further studied the separation of xylene isomers by CBMC simulations in 2014.[138] A variety of MOF candidates including JUC-77, MIL-125(Ti)- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , MIL125(Ti), Co(BDP), and MAF-X8 had been systematically screened to separate xylene isomers. Among these MOFs, MAF-X8 showed best PX selectivity, and was comparable to that of cur­ rently industrial zeolite BaX, which was mainly because MAF-X8 possessed right channel dimensions and geometry for the com­ mensurate stacking of PX molecules to occur. Recently, Chang and co-workers synthesized a Co-based MOF Co-CUK-1 used as a sorbent for selective PX adsorption.[143] The adsorptive selec­ tivity of Co-CUK-1 was shown to strongly favor PX because this isomer developed a well-defined head-to-tail molecular packing arrangement along the 1D channel of Co-CUK-1. By contrast, ordered packing of OX, MX, and EB tended to be more frus­ trated because of poorer host–guest size and shape matching.
## 6.2. Selective Adsorption Based on Molecular Sieving Effects
Compared with packing effects, sieving based on size/shape selective effects has also been shown to be viable in this regard.[144–146] The compounds UiO-66 and cyclodextrin MOF (CD-MOF) might be the two most typical representatives in this system.[3,29,145,147–151] UiO-66 manifesting a cubic 3D frame­ work comprised of two types of microporous cavities: octahedral $(11\textup{\AA})$ and tetrahedral (8 Å), and the requisite guests were acces­ sible to these cage voids through its microporous windows of roughly $5{-}7\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in diameter.[152] The kinetic diameters of these four xylene isomers (OX, EB, PX, and MX are 7.4, 6.7, 6.7, and 7.1 Å, respectively) are in close proximity to the size of the smaller tetrahedral cage. Extensive experimental and theoretical analyses had demonstrated that UiO-66 belonged to a highly OX-selective material.[145,147–149] Equimolar quaternary breakthrough experi­ ments showed that the adsorption of the bulkier OX was favored compared to its other slimmer isomers, and the elution order of xylene isomers in UiO-66 was opposite to their molecular dimen­ sion, which agreed well with the adsorption hierarchy of these isomers in a silica–UiO-66 composite.[148,149] The authors attrib­ uted such “reverse shape selectivity” to the rotational freedom of the guest molecules inside the small cavities of UiO-66.
CD-MOF represented another class of material exhib­ iting selective adsorption of ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ alkylaromatic isomers based on molecular sieving effects, which was composed of six $\gamma$ cyclodextrin $(\gamma\mathrm{{CD})}$ units and coordinated by alkali metal cations to form transverse channels in 3D.[150] This porous material was able to separate regioisomers of xylene with high separation factors of 2.67, 6.73, and 17.9 for MX/PX, OX/MX, and OX/PX, respectively, and a retention order of $\mathrm{OX}>\mathrm{MX}>$ PX under analytical high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) conditions at 298 K (Table S3, Supporting Informa­ tion). GCMC simulations revealed that OX packed in the optimum slipped geometry and had the highest affinity for the $\gamma\mathrm{{CD}}$ rings in CD-MOF-2. PX possessed smaller cross-sectional dimensions were more favorable to enter the transverse pores parallel to the $\gamma\mathrm{{CD}}$ ring, while MX and PX prevented similar positioning. In addition, quantum mechanical calculations also suggested that CD-MOF preferentially bound of styrene over EB to an isolated $\gamma\mathrm{{CD}}$ ring.[151]
## 6.3. Selective Adsorption Based on OMSs
MOFs functionalized by OMSs have also been investigated as adsorbents for the selective adsorptions of xylene iso­ mers.[153,154] Pirngruber and co-workers evaluated the ability of two unsaturated MOFs, i.e., CPO-27-Ni and HKUST-1, to separate the binary, equimolar mixtures of PX, MX, and OX in the gas phase.[153] Separation measurements showed that both CPO-27-Ni and HKUST-1 were OX-selective adsorbents and the order of elution is $\operatorname{PX}<\operatorname{MX}<\operatorname{OX}$ . However, the reasons for this preference of OX are quite different: for CPO-27-Ni, this resulted from a combination of the electrostatic effects and intrinsic OX preference of the structure, but for HKUST-1, the electrostatic interactions play a larger role due to its easily accessible OMSs and unique pore system, hence, the PX selec­ tivity is overcompensated by electrostatic effects, which overall leads to a slight preference for the OX adsorption.
Very recently, Long and co-workers had demonstrated that the well-known Co-MOF-74 facilitated the separation of the xylene isomers.[154] Qualitative multicomponent vapor-phase breakthrough experiments revealed that Co-MOF-74 could dis­ tinguish among all four molecules, and PX was eluted from the sample columns first followed by MX, EB, and finally OX, which was consistent with the trend of binding affinities determined from the single-component adsorption isotherms (Figure 14b,c). Single-crystal X-ray diffraction studies indicated that the framework favored OX due to the differential extent of interactions and packing modes of OX, EB, and MX with two adjacent ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ OMSs and the inability of PX to interact with the second metal site (Figure 14a). Moreover, Co-MOF-74 exhibited an unprecedented pore distortion upon binding of OX and EB, allowing the accommodation of additional guest molecules.
## 6.4. Selective Adsorption Based on Gate-Opening or Breathing Effects
In addition to the rigid MOF sorbents as mentioned above, flexible MOFs respond differently to specific guest molecules, which can also aid the selective separation of xylene isomers. In the experiment of adsorption separation, flexible frameworks generally undergo “breathing or gate-opening”-type structural changes. MIL-53(Al) is one of the most extensively studied flexible MOFs for ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ aromatic isomers separations.[3,45,155–159] Early work had shown that MIL-53(Al) had a pronounced pref­ erence for the OX, with a decreased selectivity in the order of $\mathrm{OX}>\mathrm{MX}>\mathrm{PX}$ at low ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ alkylaromatics concentration.[159] As evidenced by subsequent Rietveld refinements, specific interac­ tions of the closest $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ groups (in OX) with the pore walls of MIL-53 determined this preference trend. MX and PX can only interact with the pore channel’s surface to induce a framework deformation for the flexible network of MIL-53(Al) in the OXsorption process. In the following study, Song and co-workers confirmed that OX had the less negative entropy of adsorption than MX and PX, which further explained such an OX-selective breakthrough elution order.[158] Moreover, the separation perfor­ mance of MIL-53(Al) was highly dependent on total pressure. When pressure was below the “pore-opening” pressure, pores contracted so that nearly no molecules could be adsorbed. At high pressures, pores opened, and large adsorption capacity and separation factors were observed.[155]
ZIFs are a subset of MOFs with the extraordinary window flexibility and excellent chemical stability, which have also been explored extensively as potential adsorbing materials for the separation of ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}$ alkylaromatic isomers in recent years.[160–162] In spite of its small pore aperture, ZIF-8 is able to adsorb the mole­ cules that are much larger than its nominal pore size due to its extraordinary framework flexibility.[162] PX molecules could be selectively accommodated in ZIF-8 through a transitory defor­ mation of pore aperture (increased from 3.4 to $6.4\mathring\mathrm{~A}\big]$ followed by a return to the initial conformation, namely gate-opening effects, which behaved like a saloon door that opened when the guest entered the venue and closed immediately after the event ends.[161] Moreover, the quaternary breakthrough studies showed an increasing elution order of $\mathrm{OX}<\mathrm{MX}<\mathrm{EB}<\mathrm{PX}.$ , which was absolutely coherent with a molecular sieving-predicated kinetic separation mechanism.[162] In addition, ZIF-76 presented capacity of about 0.26 mmol $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for OX and 0.18 mmol $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for PX at $398\mathrm{~K~}$ , with an OX/PX selectivity of 1.1.[160]
## 7. Conclusion and Outlook
MOFs have been demonstrated to be very promising adsorbing agents in LHs molecular separation due to their functional­ ized pore surfaces, tailored structures, and structural flexibili­ ties. Research on these separations by MOFs has undergone rapid development during the past decade. In this progress report, we have summarized recent advances in exploiting MOF materials for efficient separation and purification of sev­ eral typical LHs mixtures. Also, we outlined several proposed separation mechanisms, i.e., molecular sieving effect, thermo­ dynamic equilibrium separation, kinetics driven separation, conformation driven separation, and gate opening or breathing effect, which have been demonstrated to govern the separation performances of MOF materials. Markedly, intensive studies on structure–property relationships have revealed that a single sep­ aration mechanism often met with limited success. However, if we could simultaneously and cooperatively integrate more than one of them into one MOF to form a multifunctional adsorbing material, the separation performance may result in unex­ pected improvements. For example, separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ by SIFSIX-2-Cu-i was through cooperative host–guest inter­ actions and size/shape exclusion,[82] and sorting of $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins with GeFSIX-2-Cu-i, NbFSIX-2-Cu-i, and GeFSIX-14-Cu-i was by combining molecular recognition and size-sieving mecha­ nism.[126] Simultaneously, ZIF and MIL series of MOFs with structural flexibility all manifested outstanding performance in the separation of xylene isomers. Additionally, some break­ throughs have been made by MOF materials in the effective separation of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ (SIFSIX-1-Cu), $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{6}}$ $\displaystyle\big(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ (dobdc) and UTSA-280, $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{4}/C_{3}H_{6}}$ (NbOFFIVE-1-Ni), ${\mathrm{C}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}/$ $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ (SIFSIX-3-Ni and Y-abtc), and $\mathrm{C}_{4}$ olefins $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}^{\prime}\mathbf{C}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{x}$ (Zn-BTM) mixtures, and the unprecedented separation performances of them are really encouraging.
Although amazing progresses have been made, MOFs as a kind of new absorbing materials for the separation and purifica­ tion of LHs are still in their infancy currently and some underde­ veloped issues are still needed to be considered and addressed: 1) Low thermal and mechanical stability. Design of MOFs with high stability is one of the most basic guarantee and require­ ment for their industrial applications, which is still highly in demand. 2) Ultrafine control over pore shape and size. As the first consideration, the pore size/shape of a porous material plays a determining role in selecting for special separation, which will still be one of the elementary frontiers to ponder about. 3) Rational functionalization of the pore surface and cavity. Introduction of functional groups or OMSs into pores of MOFs is crucial to modulate the chemical environments or the aperture size and shape of pore system for more efficient tuning of host–guest interactions or guest–guest interactions, including $\pi\cdot\cdot\cdot\pi$ stacking, H-bonding, van der Waals forces, intermolecular dipole and electrostatic interactions, etc. Modi­ fying or functionalizing the surface properties of MOFs is still a promising tactic to greatly enhance the selective adsorption and separation ability. 4) Making full use of and development of structural flexibility of MOFs. Flexible MOFs are capable of changing their structures to accommodate the targeted guest molecules in response to external stimuli, which are additional advantages compared with rigid porous materials for selective separation. Taking full advant e of this unique superiority will produce more fruitful and diversified research avenues in MOFs for LHs sepa Design and develop­ ment of novel rate MOFs with other e the drawbacks of the individua effects to enhance cellent perfor­ mance the selec­ tive separati and only a few acc inten­ sive effort g of novel MOF compo pecially for the sep Computer-assisted cree ar t present, more than 20 000 reported over the past decade, and few of them hosen proper absorbing materials for LH is computational simulation methodolog iently advanced to allow for screening of large databases of real and predicted MOFs structures, and can also provide some detailed microscopic information that is generally difficult or is not accessible to obtain experimentally. 7) Shaping or packing MOFs into different morphologies. In industrial application process, adsorbents are often required to be shaped or packed into given morphologies, such as agglom­ erates, tablets, batons, pellets, etc., so that to reduce the pressure drop across the adsorbent beds as well as to enhance mechanical strength and heat-shock resistance. Such morphological effect on the performance of LHs separation has been less well explored to date, but this is another issue that must be considered sooner or later. 8) Optimizing and developing of MOFs grading combi­ nation technology. As the saying goes, many hands make light work. It is very difficult to separate those multiple and com­ plex components mixtures, such as sorting of $\mathrm{C_{1}-C_{4}}$ LHs mix­ tures, entirely by only one MOF, but if we reasonably load two or more MOFs with different functionalities in same fixed bed, just like the method proposed by Long’s group in separating a 6-componet $\mathrm{CH_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}/\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}}/\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}/\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture.[78] The results always tend to be better compared with independent MOF loading. Namely, MOFs’ collaborative functions can com­ pensate fourth shortage of MOF’s individual function.
Aside from judiciously designing of functionalized MOFs with high stability and excellent separating efficiency, in-depth understanding of MOFs/adsorbents interactions and other underlying mechanisms from the viewpoint of fundamental research is also highly desired. Good insights into the structure– property relationships or separation mechanisms can aid future research to improve the separation performance of known frameworks, as well as to exploit novel MOFs based on these insights. Meanwhile, a balance should still be maintained and improved among uptake capacity and separation selectivity to overcome the barrier of “trade-off” effect in the future work. Given rapid growth in this domain and the unparalleled features of MOF materials, we believe that MOFs are capable of coping with all the obstacles that are currently faced, and will bring a bright future toward the separation and purification of LHs.
